      "id": "translation",
      "html": "<h2>English translation</h2><p class=\"translation-author\"><a href=\"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5187-5163\">Marta Fogagnolo</a></p>  <p>To the Underworld Gods. Sweetest Daphne, farewell. You lived for … years. … of fortune …</p>  "
    },
    {
      "id": "commentary",
      "html": "<h2>commentary</h2>  <p>This is an epitaph for a woman named Daphne, preceded by an invocation to the chthonic gods. The deceased is described with an attribute (ll. 2–3 <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">γλυ[κυ]|τάτη</span>) that is quite common, followed by a greeting and the verb ζάω in the second person singular, with a direct address to the deceased made even more effective by the greeting formula. The age has been lost in a lacuna. The final part of the epitaph is very fragmentary: Kaibel suggests lines 5-6 likely to be consolatory epithets (e.g. <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">τύχης ... πεπρωμεν</span>), rather than e.g. name of a male relative responsible for the building of the monument (as Alessi and Franz believed, since the first supplied at ll. 5-6 <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Ν[εοκορύτ]|ος πεπ[ρωμένυς</span>, the second at ll. 4-6 <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Εὐ]|τύχης [πατὴρ ἴδι]|ος πεπ[οίηκεν</span>), and notes a suggestion of Willamowitz that the final part might have been in verse (iambic trimeter), and even the whole, if the age were a single syllable (e.g. <span class=\"lang\" lang=\"grc\">Δάφνη γλυ[κυ]|τάτη χαῖρ[ε ν´ ζή]|σασ’ ἔτη</span>).</p>  "
    },
